er, B3 is more needed for that chow. Given B4 is exhausted (0 left), B2-B3-B4 is impossible, so B1-B2-B3 is the only B chow possible. I have B2,B2, so if I draw B3, I can use B1,B2,B3 and have one B2 left. Alternatively, I could aim for chows in W or T suits.  \n\nActually, looking at unshown tiles: W3:3 left, W7:2 left, T8:1 left, B1:2 left, B2:3 left, B5:2 left. So B3 has 3 left, good chance.  \n\nIf I discard B5, I keep B1,B2,B2 for potential B chow, and keep W3,W3 for pair, and W7,T8 for other chow possibilities. That seems reasonable. But B5 is not contributing much to chows.  \n\nBetter: discard W7? W7 has only 2 left, but it can form chows with W6,W8. Opposite player has meld [W5,W6,W7], so W6 is less available (1 left). W8 has 2 left. Still possible.  \n\nBut I think discarding a tile that is less useful for chows and not a pair candidate. My two W3s are a good pair candidate because I already have one W3 in my melded Chow, so having two W3s in hand is fine. B2s are also pair candidate.  \n\nI should discard a tile that doesn't fit with others. B5 is isolated. Discard B5 keeps B1,B2 for chow, keeps W3 pair, keeps W7,T8 for other suits.  \n\nThus:  \n\nSituation: Hand needs two more melds and a pair. Many simples available. Aim for All Simples + All Chows for 2+1=3 points, plus maybe Self-Drawn or other patterns.  \nEvaluation: Discard B5 is safe and maintains flexibility.  \nReason: B5 is not well-connected to other tiles; keeping B1,B2,B2 offers better chow potential with B3; W3 pair is valuable; W7 and T8 can form chows in their suits.  \nAnswer: Play B5"}
